  "query_preview": "..A couple of interesting features for Southeast \nAlaska for the next work week as the ridge plaguing the area \nfinally breaks down fully on Monday. An upper level low moving up \nfrom the Aleutian Chain looks to swing across a negatively tilted \nshortwave across the northern gulf. The warm sector and warm front\nassociated with this trough looks to move into the northern half \nof the panhandle first, raising snow levels briefly to well over \n10000 ft and inviting warm rain processes. This combined with \norographic lifting will result in the heaviest portion of \nrainfall, with Yakutat having a 80% likelihood of exceeding 1.5 \ninches of rain overnight on Monday. As the warm front weakens and \nbecomes occluded, rainfall drops rather dramatically to less than \na half inch during the latter portion of Monday. Winds are \nexpected to roughly follow the same story, with a barrier jet \nforming along the NE gulf coast, with up to gale force winds \nbetween Icy Cape and Cape Suckling, and strong southeasterlies \nelsewhere. \n\nAs the southwesterly energy surges over the Coast mountains,\nexpecting to see some leeside troughing which, in combination with\nthe continued ridge axis in the southern panhandle, will cause a\nsignificant southerly pressure gradient. Its during this time \n(Monday) that moderate to strong southerly breezes for Chatham \nStrait, Stephens Passage, and Lynn Canal will begin, with Skagway \npossibly exceeding 40 mph gusts.\n\nBeyond Monday, the active pattern continues, but confidence on\nindividual features drop from moderately high to moderately low.\nEven still, confidence is increasing that another system may be in\nthe works for Wednesday, bringing more rain and wind.",
